course: dictionary
lesson: 2999
title: The dungeon
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: A dungeon is a dark prison room, often underground, in a castle. You can use the words around it to check this. In «The Wind in the Willows» Toad is sent to «the deepest dungeon of all», and deepest tells you it is far down, below the ground.
explain: Stories also use a dungeon to show that someone is trapped. When a character is «locked in the dungeon», the reader knows they are stuck and need rescue. Ask yourself what the place feels like: dark, cold, and hard to escape.
explain: Games use the word in a different way. In a video or board game, a dungeon is a set of dangerous rooms or tunnels that players explore. The meaning is still about danger and hidden places, but now it is a place to play in, not a real prison.
fact: A dungeon is a dark prison room, often underground, in a castle.
fact: In a story, a dungeon often shows that a character is trapped and needs rescue.
fact: In games, a dungeon is a set of dangerous rooms or tunnels to explore.
fact: In «The Wind in the Willows», Toad is given twenty years and taken to a dungeon.
fact: By 1908 most prisoners were held in purpose-built prisons, not in castle dungeons.
exercise: «He was locked in the dungeon, far below the castle.» What is a dungeon? | A bright kitchen full of food | A tall tower with windows | *A dark prison room, often underground | A market where people sell carrots
exercise: Toad is taken to «the deepest dungeon of all». What does deepest tell you? | The dungeon is near the market | The dungeon is the newest room in the castle | The dungeon is full of happy guests | *The dungeon is the lowest and furthest down
exercise: In a video game, a player explores a dungeon. What does dungeon mean here? | A shop that sells swords and shields | A bedroom where the player sleeps | *A set of dangerous rooms or tunnels to explore | A type of computer screen
test: In «The Wind in the Willows», what is Toad given by the magistrates? | A medal for good behaviour | A new house by the river | *Twenty years in prison | A free dinner at the castle
test: Why do fairy tales often use a dungeon? | To show a river is flooding | To show the hero is lost in a forest | *To show someone is trapped and needs rescue | To show a feast is being held
exam: By 1908, where were most prisoners held? | In tree houses by the river | In castle dungeons | *In purpose-built prisons with long rows of small cells | In the market-place with the carrots
